WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.760
You know when people think about climate
change a wildlife, they probably think

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.379
about polar bears and maybe the Great
Barrier Reef. Well, we can add koalas to

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.420
them now.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.360
"As many as 3 percent of the
country's New South Wales region may have

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.720
been killed as fires continue to ravage and
destroy their habitat."

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.380
The reality is

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.700
that these flies are not normal. We're in
a war zone. So I know that there's

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.880
there's been you know loss of human life
definitely loss of property here but

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.949
what impact has this fire had, this
fire season had, on wildlife in Australia?

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:37.200
Well over 1 billion animals are thought
to have died or will soon die in the

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.219
weeks, the months ahead, because of the
current fires. That's 1 billion. And we've

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.610
never had a toll like that on our
wildlife but it seems that every few

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:49.200
days, the statistics get worse. I don't
know where it will end by the end of this

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.140
fire season.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.240
Are there specific species
that are particularly being hard hit by this?

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.940
Many many animals cannot flee these big
fires. Either they can't fly, they can't

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.890
move quickly, they can't burrow — they burn to
death. Particularly because Australia has

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.930
bulldozed half of our forests, it's hard
for wildlife to flee somewhere else and

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.409
that's why you find a lot of animals
— including koalas — are wandering into

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:17.430
people's backyards into the streets
looking for some habitat to survive in.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.780
When they panic, they climb up a tree.
That works when the fires low down.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.549
But it's almost a death sentence when the
fire consumes its crown fire, the canopy

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:32.040
fire. People have talked about flames up
to 200 feet in the air. That's 70

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.000
meters. I mean that is — our trees
generally don't grow that big. So any

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.290
wildlife, birds, or lizards that
go to the top of the trees to escape a

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:44.320
normal fire, they're sitting ducks really.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.860
So I've seen a lot of particularly hard

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:55.580
to watch videos of koalas, you know,
basically charred from the fire.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.880
Are they gonna go extinct after
this? What's the status of the koala

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.360
now, and what's gonna happen to them?

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.020
Our estimation is that koala numbers over

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.780
the last 230 years have
fallen by about 95%, and these fires will

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:11.690
be inching that up towards ninety eight
ninety nine percent over time. It's

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.220
important to remember that this bushfire
and the climate crisis — it has come

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.140
after a couple hundred years of
decimating koalas and their habitat.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.780
So for 100 years,
koalas were shot for their pelts and

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.989
there's records of 8 million koala
pelts being sold in the United States,

00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:34.730
the UK, and I think Europe up until the 1930s,
when there was a moratorium put on

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.440
shooting koalas.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.840
The term "functionally
extinct" has been thrown around in the

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.450
media during these fires in reference to
the koalas. What does

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.020
"functionally extinct" mean, and is that true?

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.100
The term "functional extinction" is

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:53.390
used for animals where there are only tens
or hundreds not even thousands of

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:58.040
animals left. So rhinos, or animals where
there's ten or twenty left. We're not

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.970
there yet for koalas. In eastern
Australia koalas are listed as a

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:06.019
threatened species but at the lowest
level they're called vulnerable. We think

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:11.000
they're now eligible to be uplisted to
being an endangered species. There are

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.959
solutions to these problems, koalas and
polar bears don't need to go extinct. And

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.260
the term "functional extinction" doesn't
help that.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.520
For people like me at home

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.840
like watching this heart-wrenching
footage, is there anything we can do to

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.400
help the situation?

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:26.960
I think the first
thing people can do around the world is

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.880
to read up about what's happening in
Australia, because if it's happening in our

00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:34.880
country, if it's not already happening in
your country, it will be in the future,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.319
of some type, as the climate gets worse
and worse. We need to convince people who

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:42.769
don't care about nature or the climate
— particularly decision makers,

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:47.480
governments, presidents, prime ministers —
you have a role in helping keep these

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.560
species alive.

